0	265	Tonio Borg (born 12 May 1957) is a Maltese lawyer, law professor, and former politician who served as the European Commissioner for Health and Consumer Policy replacing John Dalli as member of the Col
266	367	He lectures Public Law at the University of Malta, and has authored numerous books on the subject.



367	453	Political career
Borg was first elected in 1992, serving as Minister for Home Affairs.
454	568	After the Nationalist Party lost the 1996 election, he became a shadow minister to the Alfred Sant administration.
569	827	Eventually, the Nationalist Party would retake the government in 1998, wherein Borg assumed the role of Minister of Home Affairs until parliament was dissolved in 2003, in preparation for Malta's EU a
828	931	After the 2003 general election, Borg was elected, and resumed as Minister of Justice and Home Affairs.
932	980	In 2004, he was sworn as Deputy Prime Minister.

980	1084	In the 2008 general election, the Nationalist Party returned to government, albeit with a slim majority.
1085	1134	Tonio Borg was made Minister of Foreign Affairs.

1134	1232	In 2012, Borg was appointed as EU Health (and Consumer Policy) Commissioner, replacing John Dalli.
1233	1388	EU ministers majorly approved Tonio Borg's candidature, yet his conservative views on gay rights and abortion were subject to criticism from several MEPs.

1388	1520	Disagreeing with the idea of a 'potential human being', Borg sought to entrench Malta's restriction of abortion in the Constitution.
1521	1719	Whilst the proposition received support from some Labour Party MP's, most notably Marie-Louise Coleiro Preca, it was dismissed by moderate politicians in the Nationalist Party and the Labour Party.

1719	2065	Initial controversy surrounding his election as Health Commissioner centered around perceived conflicts with his portfolio, wherein Borg, a Catholic conservative-leaning Christian Democrat, would over
2066	2131	Nevertheless, Borg maintained an impartial and moderate presence.
2132	2207	The post of Maltese commissioner was succeeded by Karmenu Vella in 2014.



2207	2336	Academia
Graduating with a law degree in 1979, he would start lecturing at the University of Malta initially as a guest lecturer.
2337	2511	Eventually, in 2016, he became an assistant lecturer, until 2018 wherein he completed his PhD in Judicial Review and Administrative Law, and was promoted to senior lecturer.

2511	2555	He became associate professor in March 2020.
2556	2598	In 2024 he was promoted to full professor.
2599	2651	He lectures Constitutional, and Administrative law.

2651	2698	His PhD thesis was published as a book in 2020.
2699	2874	In it, Borg observes the field of Administrative law in Malta, highlighting potential abuses of power and the manner in which Maltese courts apply the ultra vires doctrine.



